WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.488
This is a thirty-second launch film for a desk lamp called Ember.

00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:15.667
It was made in OpenMotionStudio, from one paragraph, in about fifteen minutes. Here is every step.

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:25.211
Start at Home, and describe the film in your own words: what it's for, its beats, its length and its shape.

00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:31.040
Start creating opens a project, with your words waiting for an assistant.

00:00:33.445 --> 00:00:45.126
First, give it a look. Motions opens the MotionHub catalogue: styles designers made, each with its colours, type and animated parts.

00:00:45.126 --> 00:00:55.391
Stage Light is a dark stage with one soft light and large condensed numbers. Right for a product with facts to tell.

00:00:55.391 --> 00:01:01.850
Add only puts it in the project, with the designer's notes for the agent.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:13.090
Then bring your own material. Import files copies the two product photos into the project. The originals stay where they are.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:24.653
Now connect an assistant. Here it's the Claude Code already on this computer. It could be Codex, or any model with your own key.

00:01:24.653 --> 00:01:35.877
The brief goes. The agent reads the look's notes, looks at the photos, writes the film, then checks its own frames and fixes what fails.

00:01:35.877 --> 00:01:40.828
It even noticed that my two photos show two different lamps.

00:01:41.828 --> 00:01:52.788
Three and a half minutes later, the first cut: four scenes, thirty seconds, every line on the timeline.

00:01:54.128 --> 00:02:02.008
It's close. Two things I'd change. Instead of describing where, I draw on the picture.

00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:10.529
At twenty-eight seconds, the tagline is as loud as the name. I circle it and say what I want.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:18.866
At nine seconds, I'd like the camera to move toward the lamp. Another circle, another note.

00:02:18.866 --> 00:02:26.288
Both marks wait beside the message, each with its time. I add one line, and send.

00:02:27.288 --> 00:02:37.717
Only those two things change. The tagline is smaller and arrives a beat after Ember. The lamp shot pushes in, and its light spreads wider.

00:02:37.717 --> 00:02:43.159
Every turn is kept as a version on your disk, so you can always go back.

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:49.013
Now give it a voice. Voice lists every line of the film.

00:02:49.013 --> 00:02:54.562
Pick a voice from your own speech provider, hear it, and use it.

00:02:54.562 --> 00:02:59.937
The agent speaks each line, and the captions follow the words.

00:03:00.937 --> 00:03:06.521
Not sure about an idea? Try it. Here, a bright daytime version.

00:03:06.521 --> 00:03:10.332
It isn't Ember, so: Go back to before this.

00:03:10.332 --> 00:03:16.715
The files return to how they were, and my message comes back to the box.

00:03:18.237 --> 00:03:29.397
Last, Export MP4. The Studio checks the film, renders it on this computer, and saves it where you choose.

00:03:29.397 --> 00:03:30.566
Here it is.

